@open-design/genui-catalog — third-party notices

Every component in components/ except the Cult UI Pro ports listed in their own section at the
end of this file is a clean-room implementation written from the behaviour
descriptions in the catalog's component specs (the effect name and the one-line "What it does"
column of data/source-csv-snapshot.csv). No React Bits, 21st.dev or Kokonut UI implementation source code or component
documentation was read, copied or recalled to write them (one incidental usage example appeared
in a React Bits homepage fetch during discovery and was not used; see docs/component-gap-inventory.md), and no component depends on React,
GSAP, three.js or any other package.

The names in catalog.json (source.component, sourceUrl) record where each effect was first seen,
and source.license records that source's licence verbatim, as evidence for the licence decision.
They do not mean any code was taken from that source.

A porter appends one line per ported component below:
<id> — clean-room implementation of the effect named "<Component>" (<sourceUrl>); no upstream code.

Attribution for plain-MIT sources (licenceDecision "port-with-attribution" in catalog.json):
none. The plain-MIT effects ported so far (glyph-portal, liquid-glass-card) are clean-room
implementations from their behaviour descriptions, recorded as licenceDecision "clean-room"; no
upstream code was copied, so no upstream notice applies.

